{
  "dom_id": "step-q65858",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "What is the maximum value of $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255908/assets/bd977ea0c05962df421f60fed24ad4a68999e821379b7a5b7aa587611730357b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is $y=\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that that $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ has a maximum value of $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above inequality by $\\frac{1}{2}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$",
